  "id": "DrugBank_DB14539 - MONDO_4980",
  "verbalization": "Hydrocortisone Acetate is implicated in the pathogenesis of Atopic Eczema through its role in treating dermatitis, a condition that is closely linked to the disease. The evidence suggests that Hydrocortisone Acetate may exert its therapeutic effect by targeting inflammatory pathways associated with Atopic Eczema, as it is indicated for Dermatitis, Atopic, and is a precursor to drugs like Crisaborole, Pimecrolimus, and Dupilumab, which are also used in the management of Atopic Eczema. These drugs are typically used as topical treatments, and their indication for Atopic Eczema implies a mechanism of action that involves modulating immune responses or reducing inflammation. The shared indication for Dermatitis, Atopic across all three entities suggests a common pathway or a shared underlying mechanism in the treatment of atopic dermatitis. The presence of multiple drugs with similar indications for Atopic Eczema further supports the hypothesis that Hydrocortisone Acetate may be part of a broader therapeutic strategy for this condition.",
